#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100713 :question/id #uuid "cbd0cb3d-cf0f-54db-8cfc-c938e463e4f8" :question/math-academy-id "e-5087" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=2t^2, \\,\\,\\, y=t^2+1, \\quad t \\geq 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t^2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx=2t^2 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad  t^2 = \\dfrac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nNow substituting $t^2=\\dfrac{x}{2}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={t}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x+1.$\n\nFinally, since $t \\geq0$ and $x=2t^2,$ we must have $x \\geq 0.$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{2}x+1, \\quad x \\geq 0 .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100714 :question/id #uuid "f3d396ea-caed-51ed-a4d8-0bfc3301f2f3" :question/math-academy-id "e-5101" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=\\csc{t},\\,\\,\\, y=2\\cot{t}, \\quad 0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=x \\\\ y=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=\\frac{y}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the Pythagorean identity $\\sin^{2}{t}+\\cos^{2}{t}=1$ by $\\sin^{2}{t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t}+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} & =\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\ 1+{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\right)}^{2} & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ 1+{cot}^{2}⁡t & ={csc}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\csc{t} = x$ and $\\cot t =\\dfrac{y}{2}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{\\left(\\frac{y}{2}\\right)}^{2} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4({x}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y^2 = 4\\left( x^2-1 \\right).$\n\nFinally, since $0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ and $x=\\csc t,$ the interval on the $x$-axis is $x \\geq 1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101207 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28b55e3d-922e-5fd5-a435-05886bb984e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{\\left(\\frac{1+2x}{x}\\right)}^{2},\\,x\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272749} {:answer/id #uuid "8a657071-1650-5d6c-b5c4-92125994bb16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={\\left(\\frac{1-2x}{x}\\right)}^{2},\\,x\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273580} {:answer/id #uuid "94ae029c-717d-594a-94b3-99e945ac9df3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={\\left(\\frac{-1-2x}{x}\\right)}^{2},\\,x\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273683} {:answer/id #uuid "e5c8491d-ffbc-5e21-af51-3dd2104b993d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={\\left(\\frac{1+2x}{x}\\right)}^{2},\\,x\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274417} {:answer/id #uuid "f9e82ab8-5078-5eff-8d80-7c402c1a0adc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{\\left(\\frac{-1+2x}{x}\\right)}^{2},\\,x\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5c8491d-ffbc-5e21-af51-3dd2104b993d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={\\left(\\frac{1+2x}{x}\\right)}^{2},\\,x\\ne 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274417} :answer-field/id #uuid "67afa213-144b-5939-9e4e-014024f2077a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5acf88e6-b052-5b29-9849-a312529836f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-15678" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{t-2},\\,\\,\\,y={t}^{2},\\,t\\ne 2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{t-2} \\\\ x(t-2) & =1 \\\\ t-2 & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{x}+2 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1+2x}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substituting $t=\\frac{1+2x}{x}$ into our expression for $y={t}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={\\left(\\frac{1+2x}{x}\\right)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y={\\left(\\frac{1+2x}{x}\\right)}^{2}.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ne 2$ and $x=\\frac{1}{t-2},$ we must have $x\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny={\\left(\\frac{1+2x}{x}\\right)}^{2},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101208 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11e05f86-8f81-5b3b-8cbf-09bc7298eb8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{x+1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{y-4}{5}\\right)}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272549} {:answer/id #uuid "41070dd5-9a71-5c9d-9d49-0625efd1a709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{x-1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{y+4}{5}\\right)}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272941} {:answer/id #uuid "9c93177b-838f-57ab-b798-85239c0d52fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{x+4}{5}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{y-1}{2}\\right)}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273743} {:answer/id #uuid "a3cb9f8c-23b7-50ba-90d6-f90b45e32f80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{x+4}{5}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{y+1}{2}\\right)}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273799} {:answer/id #uuid "cbde9ca8-dffe-5a3f-aa7f-4f8da9c67b9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{x-1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{y-4}{5}\\right)}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11e05f86-8f81-5b3b-8cbf-09bc7298eb8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{x+1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{y-4}{5}\\right)}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272549} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c253336-332a-5d61-90d0-7dcad87c9060" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274655}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "20bf63df-5014-5d31-baeb-e6e95f136242" :question/math-academy-id "q-15751" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1,\\,\\,\\,y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x+1}{2} \\\\ y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{y-4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{x+1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{y-4}{5}\\right)}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\left(\\frac{x+1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{y-4}{5}\\right)}^{2}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101209 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d7810ca-e33f-5d3f-8f99-cc35e16c36f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{y}{2}\\right)}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)}^{2}=2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272648} {:answer/id #uuid "8e37f2c5-9169-53fe-b71d-d0dfd5d3531e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\left(\\frac{y}{3}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273615} {:answer/id #uuid "c4037945-4011-53b4-b0b4-00e87ba49959" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274088} {:answer/id #uuid "e44ee7b9-47c6-5cb0-a376-c176b5ec298d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{y}{3}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274406} {:answer/id #uuid "ebf8aa37-6b95-5381-b808-fe4e44945127" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{y}{2}\\right)}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e44ee7b9-47c6-5cb0-a376-c176b5ec298d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\left(\\frac{y}{3}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274406} :answer-field/id #uuid "070cfa41-26cd-55dc-a980-842b8db620c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0582dce7-ef1a-5cfc-9ee5-447a325b4a34" :question/math-academy-id "q-15752" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{y}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{y}{3}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\left(\\frac{y}{3}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101210 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85aa4d14-80d1-5752-8fd1-0411a67d3ba3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = (x - 2)^{2}, x ∈ (- ∞, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098596} {:answer/id #uuid "88ed67e5-8008-5e9a-bf16-7f21332088a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = x^{2}, x ∈ (- ∞, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098597} {:answer/id #uuid "cb64b154-ef9a-5411-b1ee-b7338acfc194" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = x + 2, x ∈ (- ∞, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098598} {:answer/id #uuid "ec57c426-a179-55be-92bb-f8bfdbc1e791" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = (x + 4)^{2}, x ∈ (- ∞, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098599} {:answer/id #uuid "ed10e3fb-a8f9-5fb6-a491-53e533208b77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = (x + 2)^{2}, x ∈ (- ∞, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098600}] :answer-field/id #uuid "553cbf31-db12-5ac2-aeb1-44860435fe50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098595}] :question/id #uuid "3962e387-0e50-5c09-a43b-458e9ce412cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-15755" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n$$\n\\displaystyle x = t - 2, y = t^{2}, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105593 :question/id #uuid "2f380683-0d93-541d-a07d-95a1d889c761" :question/math-academy-id "e-4347" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 2,-3,2 \\rangle,$ $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle -1,0,5 \\rangle,$ and $\\mathbf{c}=\\langle 1,2,3 \\rangle.$ Find $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 3-5\\cdot 2)i-(-1\\cdot 3-5\\cdot 1)j+(-1\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =-10i+8j-2k \\\\  & =⟨-10,8,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨2,-3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨-10,8,-2⟩ \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-10)+(-3)\\cdot 8+2\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-48\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105594 :question/id #uuid "b5cf98f2-087d-580c-a2f6-c37ad2af4c2c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4348" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 4,2,-1 \\rangle,$ $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 0,2,1 \\rangle,$ and $\\mathbf{c}=\\langle -1,-1,2 \\rangle.$ Find $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot (2\\cdot 2-1\\cdot (-1))-2\\cdot (0\\cdot 2-1\\cdot (-1))+(-1)\\cdot (0\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =20-2-2 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105595 :question/id #uuid "483b758a-9b14-5a72-87e5-70f27a0acf48" :question/math-academy-id "e-4349" :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{c} \\cdot (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}),$ if $2\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times 3\\mathbf{c}) = 5.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a\\cdot (b\\times 3c) & =5 \\\\ 2\\cdot [a\\cdot (b\\times 3c)] & =5 \\\\ 2\\cdot 3\\cdot [a\\cdot (b\\times c)] & =5 \\\\ a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot (b\\times a) & =-\\frac{5}{6},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand again we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot (a\\times b) & =\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106126 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94a465ca-05a5-592e-8bad-ca37a3c2aada" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-68" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102633} {:answer/id #uuid "a0d2f769-915c-58df-b7d4-bb3752ade6f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102634} {:answer/id #uuid "eac50f40-f41a-5d0a-b308-ce29d1f377ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102635} {:answer/id #uuid "00728dd2-efe2-51d0-ab82-3b59e0b1c2ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102636} {:answer/id #uuid "464182a0-c3e6-5194-b73c-9efd16f5761e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102637}] :answer-field/id #uuid "781146c0-0f2f-51f2-9e4c-ecf7837bda81" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102632}] :question/id #uuid "3536ef7b-c62d-5ac9-b71e-ecfe0e15c660" :question/math-academy-id "q-20051" :question/problem "Let $a = ⟨3, 1,-3⟩$, $b = ⟨2, 6,-2⟩$ and $c = ⟨0, 1,-4⟩$. Find $a \\cdot (b \\cdot c)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106127 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef0121b9-48b2-59dd-9889-fff75b6efdbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102639} {:answer/id #uuid "0aaf6536-05eb-54bd-a02a-e08495548f82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102640} {:answer/id #uuid "11c93f50-265e-5f32-b03a-958195c8c5d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102641} {:answer/id #uuid "30eba392-0ba7-53ce-ba87-641b3c5d3b13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102642} {:answer/id #uuid "39d77aa3-10b4-5494-9140-5ae314b6955d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102643}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ba1c3cc8-5380-5b8b-bee6-d0150e00d988" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102638} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0709d4de-7687-5bd1-81d3-9d989a778df8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376699} {:answer/id #uuid "f4c8d9bb-37c9-5b55-a1e0-665e7605b0ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376700} {:answer/id #uuid "24fc384e-6506-5fdd-854a-bb190bc2cdeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376701} {:answer/id #uuid "db568d93-3875-5af7-9432-a4e7975bebfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376702} {:answer/id #uuid "44b5b96e-b3e3-5a2a-b3fa-2d5714bb4b27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376703}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0709d4de-7687-5bd1-81d3-9d989a778df8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376699} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fe9ba40-e85f-5aca-945f-51996496427b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376698}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "83001699-71db-5629-9722-3367a7132c31" :question/math-academy-id "q-20056" :question/problem "Let $p=⟨2,-1,0⟩$, $q=⟨3,-3,2⟩$ and $r=⟨1,-4,1⟩.$ Find $r\\cdot (q\\times p).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $r\\cdot (q\\times p)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $r,$ $q,$ and $p.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot (q\\times p) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-(-4)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =((-3)\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot 2)+4(3\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 2)+(3\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot (-3)) \\\\  & =2-16+3 \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106128 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cb09803-f3ce-56d0-b3f9-23e1bde62a4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285044} {:answer/id #uuid "5976d5d8-630e-5d9c-80c0-39d8d4f64947" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285605} {:answer/id #uuid "7b2d9f03-1ac4-5377-aaed-2235dfcda0a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285926} {:answer/id #uuid "842876fd-0304-54d8-ab9d-02ba81ff1c00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286001} {:answer/id #uuid "8c4a4717-d603-58c7-99cb-3326d366dc4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286070}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b2d9f03-1ac4-5377-aaed-2235dfcda0a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285926} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cb17ee3-f413-5771-adef-20cfd3eb111a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a17a201-87cf-5140-b1f0-a5cb2ce3aff3" :question/math-academy-id "q-20058" :question/problem "Find $b\\cdot (c\\times a)$, if $-a\\cdot (3b\\times (-2c))=24.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-a\\cdot (3b\\times (-2c)) & =24 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot 3\\cdot (-2)\\cdot [a\\cdot (b\\times c)] & =24 \\\\ a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $a$ and $b$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\cdot (a\\times c) & =-4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand again we swap $a$ and $c$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\cdot (c\\times a) & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106129 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e8bb8d9-a3bb-5f41-8ede-1ac64d1b2be4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285204} {:answer/id #uuid "bb6f5fc5-825c-54cb-8f8c-ee058c85459b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286495} {:answer/id #uuid "eef31947-0a99-57ff-8618-686a5c2e30a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286980} {:answer/id #uuid "f3a78fa7-b3e6-5170-9dc3-909924cfd396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287018} {:answer/id #uuid "f992ea26-69d2-5514-84ea-1987e40856cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287068}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb6f5fc5-825c-54cb-8f8c-ee058c85459b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286495} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffd48eaf-9d9e-5aaf-ad75-c7d452ae0613" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287666}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0db80f94-e2a5-583e-875d-ce7dd7157cf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-20062" :question/problem "Find $c\\cdot (b\\times a)$, if $b\\times c=⟨2,3,0⟩$ and $a=⟨1,-2,2⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the triple scalar product \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨1,-2,2⟩\\cdot ⟨2,3,0⟩ \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =2-6+0 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $a$ and $c$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot (b\\times a)=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106130 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a7cc806-5d8c-52f5-8e9d-28150ae6a1bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285339} {:answer/id #uuid "3b0d409a-ddf1-525b-a0d4-aa58e215fa46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285343} {:answer/id #uuid "bd3e507c-3caf-5e62-ac65-0cc55ade3ef2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286515} {:answer/id #uuid "c0d377fc-8087-5c10-96ba-7988c2d59520" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286551} {:answer/id #uuid "da0f18b2-ddfb-59a1-8003-09695e06caea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286778}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a7cc806-5d8c-52f5-8e9d-28150ae6a1bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285339} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb44303e-1350-575a-9022-58d744cd9e96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287657}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "54e446ca-f780-515b-91f0-06748b3e67bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-20063" :question/problem "Find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ given that $a=⟨1,0,2⟩$ and $b\\times c=⟨-2,1,8⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Substituting given vectors into the formula for the scalar triple product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨1,0,2⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,1,8⟩ \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2)+0\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106131 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6351ad9-60eb-5c1f-b085-0679828a8f43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102663} {:answer/id #uuid "bd21b2e9-ea49-5df3-8d99-5c5fe08c5507" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102664} {:answer/id #uuid "de9107a1-a714-5163-bbc2-967ba2fe6b39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102665} {:answer/id #uuid "f05b096d-b558-5756-8dfd-2ddf5e8baaf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102666} {:answer/id #uuid "f58ada1e-09a7-5c98-bb8c-566d28d2cc28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102667}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f58ada1e-09a7-5c98-bb8c-566d28d2cc28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102667} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab5f7550-d466-5039-802c-5c3f0c6aa926" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102662}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cb9b799b-95c5-5b8c-b1f0-b4bcc39f76c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-20065" :question/problem "Given that $a=⟨0,-2,3⟩,$ $b=⟨4,-2,1⟩,$ and $c=⟨3,-1,2⟩,$ find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using the definition of the scalar triple product." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 4 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot 2-1\\cdot (-1))i-(4\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 3)j+(4\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =-3i-5j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-3,-5,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨0,-2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-3,-5,2⟩ \\\\  & =0\\cdot (-3)+(-2)\\cdot (-5)+3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119559 :question/id #uuid "98c98a56-ca57-5dc6-9dc4-c40da53806f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-3063" :question/problem "Write down the integral that gives the arc length of the curve  $\\mathbf f (t) = \\langle 3 \\sin t, \\: 2\\sin 2t \\rangle$\nfrom $t =\\dfrac {3\\pi} 2$  to $t = 2\\pi.$\n\n![](images/57/574a16e843a9c81e5fc9ddbbe2c0dc2c308ffcac4991bcbf48c7f45b4871c93b.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $\\mathbf f'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf f'(t)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{f}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡t+16{cos}^{2}⁡2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}‖{f}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡t+16{cos}^{2}⁡2t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119560 :question/id #uuid "8932c22b-465f-5a48-962c-e53730e5ba40" :question/math-academy-id "e-3064" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve $\\mathbf g (t) = \\langle 3 - 2 \\sin 2t, \\: 1 - 2\\cos 2t \\rangle$ from $t=\\dfrac \\pi 3$ to $\\pi.$\n\n![](images/29/29c97e5963d451cc389944ef113764b2d15998448e773a02bb98c062207c2860.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $\\mathbf g'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf g'(t)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{g}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}⁡2t+16{sin}^{2}⁡2t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}‖{g}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4t\\,{|}_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =4(\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119675 :question/id #uuid "118ad24e-627f-51ff-9b9c-9319c50956f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-6303" :question/problem "![](images/85/8573aedcd26cba038b2db072560cd3d6ba7676542888a2d9b8a356713eaf6724.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $\\mathbf r (t) = \\langle \\sin t ,\\: \\cos t, \\: t \\rangle$\nfrom  $t =0$ to $t = 2\\pi.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\sqrt{2}\\,t{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120272 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c60eb52-e275-5f9e-8b64-8f24c6fbbbff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{2\\cos^{2} 2t + 3\\sin^{2} 3t}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117772} {:answer/id #uuid "5068c443-8395-505c-92d8-71cea0dfc17e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\sqrt{4\\cos^{2} 2t + 9\\sin^{2} 3t}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117773} {:answer/id #uuid "748e7d72-d682-5524-af39-e923fed4a5ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\sqrt{4\\sin^{2} 2t + 9\\cos^{2} 3t}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117774} {:answer/id #uuid "7997a0c6-f7e9-5d98-aedd-32f939d3e168" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{2\\sin^{2} 2t + 9\\cos^{2} 3t}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117775} {:answer/id #uuid "7eb306df-b1bd-5be5-b18b-0285d0ccf69e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\sqrt{2\\cos^{2} 2t + 9\\sin^{2} 3t}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117776}] :answer-field/id #uuid "6e0ba63d-f616-5753-b3ec-5ef064c6cb5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117771} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc3dbeb4-85f0-5977-97bc-032932c84bc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2{cos}^{2}⁡2t+9{sin}^{2}⁡3t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391649} {:answer/id #uuid "769deadf-6f4e-5979-ab29-41e31ac33156" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡2t+9{cos}^{2}⁡3t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391650} {:answer/id #uuid "8acb4dd0-ab7b-50a4-bed8-752a2b64b388" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡2t+9{sin}^{2}⁡3t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391651} {:answer/id #uuid "4b968ecc-eb2e-5899-bdd8-779a4c1f182d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{2{cos}^{2}⁡2t+3{sin}^{2}⁡3t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391652} {:answer/id #uuid "0ec6159d-f7de-5b2f-b124-7ba4b0ab6ebc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{2{sin}^{2}⁡2t+9{cos}^{2}⁡3t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8acb4dd0-ab7b-50a4-bed8-752a2b64b388" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡2t+9{sin}^{2}⁡3t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391651} :answer-field/id #uuid "438187f3-2ea9-5adc-8e76-df9ae09801fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "994d254c-8dfb-50db-8abf-3c167a677836" :question/math-academy-id "q-33728" :question/problem "![](images/d1/d162245ac19c3ef9ae63d602fb4e188ddfd241a1a604304f6c272c3902ce1e93.png)\n\nWhich integral expression gives the arc length of the curve $g(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t⟩$ from $t=0$ to $t=\\pi ?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${g}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{g}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t⟩ \\\\ ‖{g}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}+(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡2t+9{sin}^{2}⁡3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }‖{g}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡2t+9{sin}^{2}⁡3t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120274 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80732c44-5634-545c-8ff4-349bbfb066cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e} + e^{2} - 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117784} {:answer/id #uuid "8580dd5e-760c-5889-98d9-960bbf1db32d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{2} - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117785} {:answer/id #uuid "a62f4b65-d49a-56e3-9420-a6fd0017782c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{2} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117786} {:answer/id #uuid "0730b85e-9b1c-5d8e-bd2e-6057303c06e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e^{2}} + e^{2} - 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117787} {:answer/id #uuid "1dd0a809-82f3-5432-8d1d-1a4d827a4d3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117788}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ee6767b3-a496-5ed5-83f0-c6e7926f26ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117783}] :question/id #uuid "788d951c-3d3b-5459-ac8b-6aca1943a14a" :question/math-academy-id "q-33759" :question/problem "![](images/c7/c76cff47179efaaf490430856b8db1f2b85f0d2000d34725a9c40a9e150ccf6b.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $r(t) = ⟨\\ln t, 2t, t^{2}⟩$\nfrom $t = 1$ to $t = e$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120275 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e49651a-bdf3-5daa-a8ac-b0f9a1acf337" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309453} {:answer/id #uuid "683d57e8-3476-5871-b8ce-02f1e4add2e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309550} {:answer/id #uuid "8f378059-5350-57fd-9b5f-1f9ef63dc60c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309892} {:answer/id #uuid "b4bd39a7-2f29-5a63-9c46-e2b53b0a1e5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310211} {:answer/id #uuid "d51c476d-8e1e-5c39-9e95-2b6de637ee4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310465}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4bd39a7-2f29-5a63-9c46-e2b53b0a1e5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310211} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3ec7fac-5ac8-5f09-95e8-e286bea551a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311408}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d7f25b66-94f4-5ecd-88ef-6b78d26ced87" :question/math-academy-id "q-33779" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve $$\n\\displaystyle f(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}{t}^{2}⟩\n$$\nfrom $t=1$ to $t=3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${f}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{f}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\sqrt{3}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{3}t⟩ \\\\ ‖{f}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{3}t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)+3{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $t>0$ for $t\\in [1,3].$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}‖{f}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =9-1 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120281 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7002bf97-f2f4-584e-9358-9901bcdc36c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+2t+5}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312858} {:answer/id #uuid "c02b3e07-f8b1-5a36-8107-54957d2ec5d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+2t+2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313481} {:answer/id #uuid "db80e8bf-9842-5c6d-bd83-4583ec9425fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+2t+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313702} {:answer/id #uuid "e797d87f-61ea-5692-b189-9f791eb47e1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+2t+6}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313811} {:answer/id #uuid "f08c0c47-5ca9-5225-ad0d-e2d99574aadd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}(t+2)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313902}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e797d87f-61ea-5692-b189-9f791eb47e1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+2t+6}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313811} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc1df9aa-037a-519b-b947-a899bc624f42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314526}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3bc45ddc-02bd-5a1e-bfa3-f99f90a0c552" :question/math-academy-id "q-33862" :question/problem "Which integral  expression gives the arc length of the curve $f(t)={e}^{t}(\\,i+2\\,j+t\\,k)$ from  $t=1$ to $t=2?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${f}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{f}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t{e}^{t})\\,k \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\,i+2{e}^{t}\\,j+(t{e}^{t}+{e}^{t})\\,k \\\\ ‖{f}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{({e}^{t})^{2}+(2{e}^{t})^{2}+({e}^{t}(t+1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+4{e}^{2t}+{e}^{2t}({t}^{2}+2t+1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}(1+4+{t}^{2}+2t+1)} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+2t+6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}‖{f}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+2t+6}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120527 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75e5fa3c-091c-520e-8479-d218155399c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343254} {:answer/id #uuid "8b51d751-2961-50bb-98bc-c99dea65a8e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343452} {:answer/id #uuid "8baa903e-69cf-5fab-bf1d-52f2ba066f34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343455} {:answer/id #uuid "c0402a4e-bae1-581e-b109-bffe3d5de024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343924} {:answer/id #uuid "deebeadd-1734-5b4a-bc84-8d64bed65493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344193}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8baa903e-69cf-5fab-bf1d-52f2ba066f34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343455} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0be3d9a-49eb-5fc8-91bf-4c96b81633a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344886}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d67d847a-8cc9-599e-9782-16c3ef20921d" :question/math-academy-id "q-77065" :question/problem "![](images/b6/b614e2c3e6abf0d7ddb8399aa7ea78b7a0e618a965ee7b5481edf08310a45031.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $g(t)=⟨{t}^{3}-3t,\\,3{t}^{2}⟩$ from $t=1$ to $t=2?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${g}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{g}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-3t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2}-3,\\,6t⟩ \\\\ ‖{g}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(3{t}^{2}-3)^{2}+(6t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}-18{t}^{2}+9+36{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+18{t}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3{t}^{2}+3)^{2}} \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}‖{g}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(3{t}^{2}+3)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =({t}^{3}+3t){|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(8+6)-(1+3) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120528 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42dd07b3-4065-530a-88aa-6e7a0aca511d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342795} {:answer/id #uuid "78d7838b-fe95-5cad-a4c4-269d7bdb904b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343282} {:answer/id #uuid "881ebcca-4863-55f5-9890-dcd0a9df648a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343415} {:answer/id #uuid "8eab15db-b045-510e-8571-aaaaa67b97ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343486} {:answer/id #uuid "fee32fe0-e17a-5ba1-bc6c-a8f5d62a18d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344482}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8eab15db-b045-510e-8571-aaaaa67b97ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343486} :answer-field/id #uuid "82eb78e8-3d58-5365-9450-5b95abad2149" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344723}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c0bd5879-0033-526b-bc0b-c6b5d407bf6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-77066" :question/problem "![](images/9b/9b83db0bb11b8e4bbcc4b04b7bf696879d4d7f4732d6aef7d005f9da2b625526.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $g(t)=⟨2{t}^{3/2},\\,3t⟩$ from $t=0$ to $t=3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${g}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{g}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3/2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\sqrt{t},\\,3⟩ \\\\ ‖{g}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(3\\sqrt{t})^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9t+9} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{t+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}‖{g}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}3\\sqrt{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}3(t+1)^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2(t+1)^{3/2}{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =16-2 \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124287 :question/id #uuid "5791bd77-96c7-571d-b8b5-76cb62eb0a9d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4228" :question/problem "The level curves of the function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{x^2+2y^2-2}{x^2}$ are given by $f(x,y) = c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $c=1$ gives a circle\n\nII. $c=0$ gives an ellipse\n\nIII. $c=2$ gives a hyperbola" :question/worked-solution "Setting $f(x,y) = c$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2}{{x}^{2}} & =c \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2 & =c{x}^{2} \\\\ (1-c){x}^{2}+2{y}^{2} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c = 1,$ then the level curve is $y^2 = 1,$ which consists of two lines.\n\n- If $c = 0,$ then the level curve is $x^2+2y^2=2,$ which is an ellipse.\n\n- If $c = 2,$ then the level curve is $2y^2-x^2=2$ which is a hyperbola.\n\nSo, only statements II and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124288 :question/id #uuid "618e7097-38d3-5167-a9d2-5d5a3fc85e0a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4229" :question/problem "Sketch the level curves of the surface $z=e^{x^2-y^2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The level curves for the function $f(x,y)=e^{x^2-y^2}$ take the form\n\n$$\ne^{x^2-y^2} = c.\n$$\n\nNote that we must have the constraint $c\\gt 0$ since $e^x \\gt 0$ for all $x.$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}=c \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡c \\\\ {x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡c.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can split this into three cases, depending on the sign of $\\ln c.$\n\n- Case 1: $\\ln c \\gt 0.$ In this case, we have the family of hyperbolas $x^2-y^2 = \\ln c.$\n\n- Case 2: $\\ln c = 0.$ In this case, we have the lines $y=\\pm x.$\n\n- Case 3: $\\ln c < 0.$ In this case, we have the family of hyperbolas $y^2 - x^2 = -\\ln c.$\n\nTherefore, the level curves are as follows:\n\n![](images/5f/5f2ec43639cb31e03cc4a00e43969c7f0333c098fc4b366ea1609772faabfda5.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124362 :question/id #uuid "fe87d9e4-6445-5ee7-9dc7-6ab8fbb165c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-5968" :question/problem "Find the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $G\\big(\\sqrt{5}, 1\\big)$ for the function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{12y}{x^2-y^2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $G$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n  f\\big(\\sqrt{5}, 1\\big)&= \\dfrac{12(1)}{\\big(\\sqrt{5}\\big)^2 - 1^2} = 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $G\\big(\\sqrt{5}, 1\\big)$ is the $3$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=3.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{12y}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & =3 \\\\ 4y & ={x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-4y-4 & =-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-({y}^{2}+4y+4) & =-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-(y+2)^{2} & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $G\\big(\\sqrt{5}, 1\\big)$ is the hyperbola $x^2 - (y+2)^2 = -4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124888 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2eeacf93-e554-58a5-b0b0-b69b6c92cd8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7c/7c4530dc74747fc0bdae0ca4c8608a72dbf03ca413d4479c8a4b9224246ac01f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321510} {:answer/id #uuid "65ab1147-95f7-57c6-a0f6-195188d68f77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b4/b4642ea9b585ac066f2ed904b1bee4df7d1ff3449ba2d09afca8364b58f1dcba.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321935} {:answer/id #uuid "6ceac803-4898-591d-8209-d1d92026603a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ae/ae78f21487e5883b278f0b62a92b2b44ffe8e83e9bd942cf6c94ed4c46d18c67.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321994} {:answer/id #uuid "88bcb675-5baf-5bc6-9fec-a149e6d8836e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/22/22ff469fae1e93c0d805e7443c110c8c56b4f005b0ce2f4a641f19a23dfa3135.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322239} {:answer/id #uuid "fe7b2229-4ac4-5665-affc-5be5302d12bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e4/e49f976cd92eac7780062c7013a29e6acb737d4496a1b61955d7c2ceac96084a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323153}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2eeacf93-e554-58a5-b0b0-b69b6c92cd8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7c/7c4530dc74747fc0bdae0ca4c8608a72dbf03ca413d4479c8a4b9224246ac01f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321510} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3f7dd4d-866d-5387-af83-8c052dcfbb2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323552}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f48129ca-8a2b-5a4f-8600-d7fd45c7c512" :question/math-academy-id "q-35520" :question/problem "Which of the following represents the level curves of $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "The level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equations \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nNotice that the range of $f(x,y)$ is $[0,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider only level curves for $c\\ge 0.$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =c \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={c}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives us a family of circles centered at the origin. Therefore, the correct graph is as follows:\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/41/413a11a52ed6a4fc76c2848867d664a63fce24dd39088cdd5826b576d8e7b09c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124890 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6571627b-c3c9-57ac-a7a9-b372d56890b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459841} {:answer/id #uuid "26bbe0f6-1b23-5589-8774-2f5cfa12fa6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459842}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26bbe0f6-1b23-5589-8774-2f5cfa12fa6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459842} :answer-field/id #uuid "c79d8b74-1e96-5e2c-897c-c7e2b649cdf9" :answer-field/key "$c=1$ gives a hyperbola" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460787} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16915dd8-a6f1-553d-81d9-5ac7d21852c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459843} {:answer/id #uuid "134336d5-9911-58d2-9ee4-0effc36ebd9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459844}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16915dd8-a6f1-553d-81d9-5ac7d21852c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459843} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc06950a-f3b8-557c-ad26-06758c78ab74" :answer-field/key "$c=2$ gives a parabola" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460788} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d64a178b-7461-5e09-a004-effd84d13a2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459845} {:answer/id #uuid "3755b067-81f0-5479-9950-bef82bc135bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3755b067-81f0-5479-9950-bef82bc135bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459846} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5dfddb0-8827-5207-8af4-b1cf45ab0f2c" :answer-field/key "$c=3$ gives an ellipse" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460789}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b428de76-1b52-5416-9b1c-9157ab5ea778" :question/math-academy-id "q-35534" :question/problem "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =c \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+1 & =c({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) \\\\ (c-2){x}^{2}+c{y}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=1$ then the level curve is ${y}^{2}-{x}^{2}=1,$ which is a hyperbola.\n\n- If $c=2$ then the level curves are $y=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},$ which consists of two lines.\n\n- If $c=3$ then the level curve is ${x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}=1,$ which is an ellipse.\n\nSo, only statements “$c=1$ gives a hyperbola” and “$c=3$ gives an ellipse” are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124972 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aed45092-c23f-501b-9c83-a11c92fd13d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x^{2} + \\frac{y^{2}}{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122433} {:answer/id #uuid "f875a957-c838-58fb-8bbc-11895a74e524" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x^{2} - \\frac{y^{2}}{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122434} {:answer/id #uuid "3a6a885f-6033-57f1-b75f-b331291c5369" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x^{2} - y^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122435} {:answer/id #uuid "4de3f5d1-2b76-5165-9980-ffb243152fde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x^{2} + y^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122436} {:answer/id #uuid "692fa8ac-874d-5235-9ad6-4c67f6177b2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} - 4x^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122437}] :answer-field/id #uuid "98e7758c-2bb3-5f6c-89bd-161adc169063" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122432}] :question/id #uuid "52be6cdb-52ca-5176-afa4-871eaa833286" :question/math-academy-id "q-36340" :question/problem "Find the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $P(0, 1)$ for the function $f(x, y) = 1 - 4x^{2} - y^{2}$."}]}
